 patterns like All Pungs (6 points) if I get another Pung. With two Pungs already, a third Pung would give All Pungs. Also, I have B4 pair, so if I Pung B4, I'd have three Pungs and one Chow, which qualifies for All Pungs? All Pungs requires four Pungs/Kongs, so I'd need four. Currently I have two Pungs and one Chow, so I need two more Pungs to get All Pungs. That's unlikely with only 4 tiles left. \n\nBetter to aim for simple win with 8+ points. Current patterns: From melds, W1 Pung is Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point), B1 Pung is Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point), B3-B4-B5 Chow is All Chows? Not necessarily. I also have no honors in hand except F3 and F4, so if I discard them, I could get No Honors (1 point) and All Simples (2 points) if I win with only suited tiles. But my melds include W1 (terminal) and B1 (terminal), so All Simples requires no terminals or honors, so I cannot get All Simples. So patterns so far: Pung of Terminals or Honors for W1 and B1 (2 points total), maybe others like Concealed Pungs? My Pungs are exposed from Peng actions, so not concealed. \n\nI need at least 8 points to win. Currently, I have 2 points from two Pung of Terminals or Honors. I could get more patterns: If I win by self-drawn, that's 1 point. If I have a pair of B4, that's not a pattern. I might get All Chows if my last meld is a Chow? But I have two Pungs, so not All Chows. I need additional patterns like Mixed Triple Chow? Unlikely. \n\nGiven the complexity, I should discard a tile that is safe to avoid giving others points, while keeping tiles that can form a meld quickly. B4 is my pair, so I keep it. T2 can form Chow with T1/T3/T4. F3 and F4 are honors, and discarding them might reduce my chance for Pung of Terminals or Honors, but I already have two such Pungs. If I draw a third F3 or F4, I could get another Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point) and maybe Two Dragon Pungs? But F3 and F4 are winds, not dragons, so no. \n\nLooking at opponents' melds: Across player