, then draw a duplicate of T2, F3, or F4 to form a pair and win. \n\nAlternatively, if I keep B4 pair and draw T1 or T3 or T4 to form Chow with T2, then I have a Chow meld, and I win with B4 pair. That seems more straightforward: I need one tile to complete a Chow with T2. T2 can form Chow with T1-T2-T3 or T2-T3-T4. T1 has 2 left, T3 has 4 left, T4 has 4 left. So good chances. \n\nSo I should keep T2 and B4 pair. Then discard either F3 or F4. Which is safer? F3 is West Wind, my Seat Wind. In Mahjong, players often discard their own Seat Wind early because it's less useful for others? Actually, others might need it for Pung if it's their Prevailing Wind or Seat Wind. Prevailing Wind is East (F1), so F3 is not prevailing. For other players: Next player's Seat Wind? Not given, but typically, if I am West, next player might be North, previous might be South, across might be East? But not sure. Generally, discarding F3 might be safer than F4 because F4 (North) might be someone's Seat Wind. Also, from history, F2 has been played by across player, F4 played by me earlier, F3 not played. So F3 might be less expected. \n\nI think discarding F3 is reasonable.\n\nReason: I need to complete a Chow with T2 to form my fourth meld while keeping the B4 pair. Discarding F3 removes an honor tile that is less likely to be needed by opponents (since it's my Seat Wind and not Prevailing Wind), and preserves the flexibility to draw T1, T3, or T4 for a Chow. Keeping B4 maintains my pair. This move aims for a quick win with minimal risk.\n\nAnswer: Play F3"}
